course: dictionary
lesson: 11322
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The words around the word are its clues. «At a crosswalk you wait for the cars to stop and make eye contact with the drivers, because some drivers do not yield (let you go first).» The clues here are cars, drivers and crosswalk, so yield means to let others go first.
explain: Yield can also mean to give up or stop fighting. If a story says the rebels finally yielded after a long fight, the clue is fight: they stopped fighting and let the other side win. Yield can also mean to bend or break when pushed hard, as in a branch that will yield under heavy snow.
explain: On a farm or in science, yield means how much something produces. «The field gave a good yield of wheat» means the field produced a lot of wheat. As a noun, a yield is that amount, so you can say a good yield or a poor yield.
explain: Words can also change from place to place. «A give way or yield sign means drivers must slow down and let other traffic go first.» In the USA they say yield; in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand they say give way. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "drivers must slow down and let other traffic go first" still makes sense.
fact: Yield is a verb and a noun: you can yield, or you can talk about a yield.
fact: Yield means to let others go first, to give up, to produce something, or to bend or break when pushed.
fact: «Yield» as a noun means the amount something produces, such as the amount of wheat from a field.
fact: A yield sign means slow down and let others go first. It is a road sign used in the USA and Canada.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, the same idea is called give way.
exercise: «A give way or yield sign means drivers must slow down and let other traffic go first.» What does yield mean on this sign? | Drive as fast as you can | Turn around and go back | *Let other traffic go first | Stop and park for a rest
exercise: «After a long fight, the rebels finally yielded.» What does yielded mean? | Grew a big crop | Painted the walls | Ran faster | *Gave up and let the other side win
exercise: «The field gave a good yield of wheat this summer.» What does yield mean here? | A yellow flower | To bend when pushed | *The amount a field produces | To let cars go first
test: «The apple tree will yield fruit in the autumn.» What does yield mean here? | To give up | *To produce | To let others go first | To bend under weight
test: «The branch began to yield under the heavy snow.» What does yield mean here? | *To bend or break when pushed hard | To give up and stop fighting | To let others go first | To produce a crop
exam: Which sentence uses yield to mean the amount something produces? | The branch began to yield. | Drivers must yield at the crossing. | *The farm had a good yield of potatoes this year. | The soldiers refused to yield.
